.TH GMAKE 1.SH NAMEgmake \- GNU make.SH SYNOPSIS\fBgmake\fR[options] [target ...].SH DESCRIPTION\fBgmake\fRis GNU Make 3.67, under a name of its own beside OS-9's\fBmake\fR.\fRIt reads\fBMakefile\fRin the current directory, or the file\fB\-f\fRnames, and remakes each target that is older than what it depends on, byrunning the commands under it.  Each command line starts with a tab..PPHere each command line is run as a program, not through a shell, so ashell's built-in commands are not available in a recipe:\fBecho made it\fRfails with\fBecho: Command not found\fR.\fRGive it programs..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-f\fI file\fRread\fIfile\fRas the makefile..TP 12\fB\-C\fI dir\fRchange to\fIdir\fRfirst..TP 12\fB\-n\fRprint the commands without running them..TP 12\fB\-k\fRkeep going past a target that fails..TP 12\fB\-i\fRignore errors from commands..TP 12\fB\-s\fRdo not echo the commands..TP 12\fB\-t\fRtouch the targets instead of remaking them..TP 12\fB\-q\fRrun nothing; the exit status says whether everything is up to date..TP 12\fB\-p\fRprint the rules and variables it knows..TP 12\fB\-r\fRno built-in rules..TP 12\fB\-I\fI dir\fRlook in\fIdir\fRfor included makefiles..TP 12\fB\-e\fRenvironment variables override the makefile's..TP 12\fB\-o\fI file\fB\fR, \fI\-W\fB file\fRtreat\fIfile\fRas very old, or as brand new..TP 12\fB\-d\fRdebugging output..TP 12\fB\-h\fRthe full option list, with the long spellings..SH SEE ALSOmake